---
// Spec format: what a screen is made of (a JSON spec of contract components), the budgets the panel checks (catalogue
// budget, lib/counts.ts), how a prop binds to Home Assistant or to a feed (the panel.<feed> entities a source fills), and
// what a tap does (a node's "on": event -> action, the closed whitelist), the deck that lists a panel's pages. Moved
// from Make screens with AI, which now links here. The full guide is the firmware's own (renderer/tools/gen_agents.mjs,
// served here as /agents.md by scripts/llms.mjs).
import Docs, { type Heading } from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Count from '../../components/Count.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/nav';
import schema from '../../../../renderer/catalogue/schema.json';

const headings: Heading[] = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'spec', text: 'A screen is a spec' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'bind', text: 'Binding to Home Assistant' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'act', text: 'Doing things' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'feeds', text: 'Feeds' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'limits', text: 'What the panel checks' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'deck', text: 'The deck' },
];
const spec = {
  v: 1, id: 'bin_day', name: 'Bin day', rev: 1, role: 'page',
  header: { label: 'Bin day' },
  layout: 'list',
  nodes: [
    { c: 'hero_block', p: { n: { e: 'sensor.bin_days', s: '2' }, label: 'days to bin day', line: { e: 'sensor.bin_next', s: 'Recycling · Tue' } } },
    { c: 'section_header', p: { title: 'next collections' } },
    { c: 'list_row', p: { title: 'Recycling', trailing: 'value', value: { e: 'sensor.bin_recycling', s: 'Tue 27 Oct' } } },
  ],
};
// a light row that switches the light: its props bound to the entity, its toggle event sent as an action
const row = {
  c: 'list_row',
  p: { title: { e: 'light.garage', fmt: 'name', s: 'Garage light' }, icon: 'bulb', trailing: 'toggle', sub: { e: 'light.garage', fmt: 'light', s: 'On' }, on: { e: 'light.garage', fmt: 'on', s: 'on' }, state: { e: 'light.garage', fmt: 'avail' } },
  on: { toggle: { do: 'light.switch', e: 'light.garage' } },
};
const fmts = (schema.spec as { fmt: string[] }).fmt;
const layouts = (schema.spec as { layouts: string[] }).layouts;
const feeds = Object.keys((schema.feeds as { list: Record<string, unknown> }).list);
---

<Docs edit="site/src/pages/inside/spec.astro" title="Spec format" description="How a Facet screen is written: a short JSON spec of components, bound to Home Assistant, checked by the panel." group="Inside Facet" current={url('/inside/spec/')} headings={headings}>
  <p class="lede">
    Every Facet screen, the factory ones included, is a short JSON spec: a header, a layout and a list of components,
    each with its props. There are no positions, sizes, colours or fonts in a spec; those come from the tokens.
  </p>

  <h2 id="spec">A screen is a spec</h2>
  <CodeBlock json={spec} label="Spec · JSON" copyLabel="Copy spec" />
  <p>
    <code>c</code> names a component and <code>p</code> holds its props. The factory specs are
    <Count k="specMinKB" /> to <Count k="specMaxKB" /> KB each.
    The components a spec can name, and every prop of each, are on <a href={url('/components/')}>Components</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>layout</code> is one of {layouts.map((l, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}<code>{l}</code></>)}: list is one scrolling
    column of rows, center one component in the middle, cards the card grid. The header's Back button is the panel's own:
    every screen but the first has it. The whole format, every component's props and which actions each one takes are in
    the <a href={url('/agents.md')}>agent guide</a>, the same one a panel serves; the JSON Schema of a spec is
    <a href={url('/spec.schema.json')}>spec.schema.json</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bind">Binding to Home Assistant</h2>
  <p>
    A prop can be a fixed value or bound to an entity: <code>{'{"e": "sensor.bin_days"}'}</code> shows its state,
    <code>{'{"e": "light.ceiling", "a": "brightness"}'}</code> one of its attributes, and <code>s</code> is what a
    preview shows instead. The screen updates by itself when the entity changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>fmt</code> turns the state into what a prop wants: <code>on</code> for a switch, <code>name</code> the entity's
    name, <code>light</code> a light's state line, <code>avail</code> the unavailable look, <code>pct</code> a percentage.
    All of them: {fmts.map((f, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}<code>{f}</code></>)}. <code>map</code> turns states into words
    (<code>{'{"on": "Open", "off": "Shut"}'}</code>, <code>else</code> for the rest) and <code>tpl</code> puts the value
    in a line (<code>{'"{} °C"'}</code>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="act">Doing things</h2>
  <p>
    A component does something when it is tapped through its node's <code>on</code>: each event it sends (a toggle's
    <code>toggle</code>, a row's <code>tap</code>, <code>long</code> for a long press) names one action. This row shows a
    light and switches it:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock json={row} label="Node · JSON" copyLabel="Copy node" />
  <p>
    Each component and event takes only certain actions, and the panel refuses the rest with what it does take
    (<code>list_row tap allows nav.open, nav.back, light.toggle</code>). The actions are on
    <a href={url('/inside/schema/#actions')}>Schema</a>; which ones each component takes is in the
    <a href={url('/agents.md')}>agent guide</a>. Note the two <code>on</code> keys: inside <code>p</code> it is the List row's
    switch, bound to the light; on the node it is what the events do.
  </p>

  <h2 id="feeds">Feeds</h2>
  <p>
    Some screens need more than an entity's state: a forecast, a calendar's events, the energy used today. A feed is an
    entity of the panel's own (<code>panel.weather</code>, <code>panel.calendar</code>, ...) that a source fills, from
    Home Assistant or worked out on the panel, and the screen binds to it like any entity. The panel has these feeds:
    {feeds.map((f, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}<code>{f}</code></>)}.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">What the panel checks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Components and tokens only.</strong> A spec can only name components the firmware ships. Spacing, type, colour and motion come from the tokens, in light and dark, so a spec cannot pick an off-grid gap or an unreadable colour.</li>
    <li><strong>Budgets.</strong> A screen is capped at <Count k="nodes" /> components, <Count k="binds" /> entity bindings and <Count k="specKB" /> KB, so it builds fast on the panel. A panel holds up to <Count k="deckScreens" /> screens.</li>
    <li><strong>Checked before it is kept.</strong> The panel checks a spec before it keeps it and answers with what is wrong and where (an unknown prop, a value out of range). Nothing it refuses replaces what was there.</li>
    <li><strong>Layout rules.</strong> One hero per screen, rows that touch, more space between groups: the layout applies them, not the spec.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="deck">The deck</h2>
  <p>
    The deck is the panel's list of pages: Home and its cards, then the screens you swipe to, in order, each with its
    settings. An agent or Home Assistant can send one. The routes and
    actions that take specs and decks are on <a href={url('/inside/schema/')}>Schema</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
